# EDGE_PROXY=cloudflare — the instance sits behind Cloudflare (proxied DNS or a
# Cloudflare Tunnel). Imported inside the global `servers` block of
# ops/Caddyfile. Only set this when every outside request really does come
# through Cloudflare: it trusts the Docker bridge range, so on a host where
# direct visitors also arrive from the bridge gateway (Docker Desktop, rootless
# Docker, IPv6 via docker-proxy) they could set their own Cf-Connecting-Ip.
#
# zekktris.com is proxied by Cloudflare, so the peer that connects to
# Caddy is always a Cloudflare edge node, never the user. Until this
# block existed, X-Real-IP was set from {remote_host} — the edge — for
# EVERY request, and getClientIp() (which trusts X-Real-IP first)
# believed it. So everything keyed on the client IP was really keyed on
# Cloudflare: per-IP rate limits shared across every user behind the same
# edge node, IP bans that would ban a slice of Cloudflare rather than a
# person, and the wrong addresses in audit logs and the sessions UI.
#
# Cloudflare's published edge ranges (www.cloudflare.com/ips-v4 and
# /ips-v6, fetched 2026-09-04). They change rarely, and if Cloudflare
# adds one, requests from it are simply untrusted and client_ip falls
# back to the edge address — it degrades to the old behaviour rather
# than opening a spoofing hole. Re-fetch when that happens.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-09-10): production does NOT reach Caddy from a
# Cloudflare edge. It arrives through a Cloudflare TUNNEL — cloudflared
# runs on the host (not in this compose file) and connects to the
# published port, so every request's peer is the Docker bridge gateway
# (172.19.0.1 at the time). The Cf-Warp-Tag-Id header in the access log is
# the tell. With only the edge ranges below trusted, that peer was
# untrusted and client_ip resolved to 172.19.0.1 for EVERY user — one IP
# for the whole instance, which is worse than the edge-IP problem this
# block was first written to fix.
#
# So Docker's bridge pool, 172.16.0.0/12, is trusted too. That covers the
# tunnel connector wherever Docker places its network, while a LAN client
# (192.168.x.x) hitting the published port directly stays untrusted and
# cannot forge its address. Other containers on this host could — but they
# are already inside the machine. The edge ranges stay, so a later switch
# to proxied DNS without the tunnel keeps working.
#
# Still deliberately NOT `private_ranges`: that would include 192.168/16
# and 10/8 and let any machine on the LAN forge its own source address.
trusted_proxies static \
 172.16.0.0/12 \
 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 \
 141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 \
 197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 \
 104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22 2400:cb00::/32 \
 2606:4700::/32 2803:f800::/32 2405:b500::/32 2405:8100::/32 \
 2a06:98c0::/29 2c0f:f248::/32
# Read the client from CF-Connecting-IP instead of the default
# X-Forwarded-For. Cloudflare OVERWRITES this header with the real
# connecting address, so unlike the leftmost X-Forwarded-For entry it
# cannot be forged by the client. It is only consulted for requests that
# arrived FROM a trusted proxy, so anyone hitting the origin directly
# with a handcrafted CF-Connecting-IP is untrusted and Caddy uses their
# actual peer address.
client_ip_headers Cf-Connecting-Ip
# A no-op while the header above carries exactly one address, but if
# X-Forwarded-For is ever added back to that list, right-to-left parsing
# is what stops the client-controlled leftmost entry being believed.
trusted_proxies_strict
